  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1251 AM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nThe main story for the upcoming week remains the dangerous late\nseason heat wave that will begin to impact parts of the area \ntoday then the entire region from Tuesday through at least \nWednesday (and likely beyond). Ensemble guidance continues to \nshow a 596-600 dm H5 ridge becoming centered over the Mid-South \ntoday through Friday. Temperatures at 850 mb will be around \n19-22C, which is near maximum values for this time of year. \n\nToday, the strongest ridging will be located over southeast MO\nand far west KY, where high temperatures will reach the lower to\nmiddle 90s. With dew point temperatures in the lower to middle\n70s, peak heat index values will reach around 105F. Have\nexpanded the inherited Heat Advisory for today to include\nlocations along and south of a line from Mayfield KY to Scott\nCity MO to Van Buren MO. \n\nBeginning Tuesday, the ridge will expand to become centered over\nthe entire region, with high temperatures in the middle to upper\n90s forecast for most of the area, with dew point temperatures\nin the lower 70s. This will yield heat index values around 105\ndegrees Tuesday and Wednesday across the entire region. Opted to\nextend the inherited Heat Advisory for the entire area from \nTuesday through Wednesday evening. Depending on how quickly dew \npoint temperatures draw down, the Heat Advisory may be extended \nthrough Thursday with later forecast updates. \n\nThe late season nature of the this heat wave has raised our \nlevels of concern for health impacts. This is due to more\nactivities taking place outdoors (school events, festivals,\nsporting events, outdoor workers, etc.) when conditions would \nusually be much cooler by the middle of September. Those \nplanning to participate in outdoor activities this week should \nbe prepared for the heat or should consider altering plans to \nminimize exposure to the heat. Looking at historical records, \nthis is latest in the season we can find that a Heat Advisory \nhas been issued for our forecast area in at least 20 years \n(which is farthest back we can find records for Heat Advisory \nissuance), so there is nothing typical about this level of heat \nand humidity in this part of September.\n\nModel guidance continues to trend slower to break down this\nridge, and high temperatures in the lower to middle 90s will\nlikely linger through next weekend. However with continued dry\nconditions, dew point will begin to draw down into the 60s,\nwhich will limit heat index values to the middle to upper 90s.\nAdditionally, the return of mentionable low-end precipitation \nchances has also been delayed until Sunday at the earliest, so\ndrought impact concerns will also increase during this time.",
